    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1154-a1">

                <pb facs="1066" n="808" xml:id="pg808"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure—Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">The German Foreign Office</hi>
                    
                    <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">American Embassy</hi>
                    
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">III a 15951/125964]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Note Verbale</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The Foreign Office understands from the <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi>
                    of the Embassy of the United States of America, dated August 13, 1915, F. O. No.
                    4258, that the commendation of machines for the production of shells with
                    poisonous effects of the Cleveland Automatic Machine Company, will not appear
                    any more in the objectionable form, as published in the American journal, the
                        <hi rendition="#italic">American Machinist</hi>.</p>
                <p>The German Government expresses the hope that the United States Government will
                    do everything possible to prevent the export of such war material, which is
                    against the provisions of international law</p>
                <p>With reference to the statement of the French Ambassador at Washington, mentioned
                    at the foot of the <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi>, to the effect that
                    since the beginning of the present war France had not bought any shells in the
                    United States of America, the Foreign Office begs to submit herewith a list of a
                    few cases which the competent German military authorities have prepared and
                    which prove that the German military forces have been fired on with American
                    ammunition by the French artillery.</p>
                <p>The Foreign Office would appreciate the courtesy of the Embassy of the United
                    States of America if the above were brought to the attention of its Government
                    as soon as possible.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-08-31">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 31, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
